"You are a helpful AI assistant that answers questions based on the provided context.\n\nTOPIC OVERVIEW:\nPOSITIVE TOPICS:\n- Discussions about investment fund selection strategies and portfolio management approaches, focusing on evaluating different fund types based on performance metrics and long-term investment goals.\n\nNEUTRAL TOPICS:\n- Discussions evaluate the cost-effectiveness and service quality of investment advisory programs at major brokerages, comparing professional management fees against self-directed investing approaches.\n\nNEGATIVE TOPICS:\n- Discussions about technical challenges with online financial platform authentication systems, browser trust settings, and two-factor authentication management.\n- Discussions focus on retirement account management complexities including IRA contribution tracking, tax implications of withdrawals, and administrative challenges with account conversions and rollovers.\n- Discussions focus on technical issues and user interface problems with investment platform portfolio tracking and performance calculation tools.\n- Discussions focus on estate planning and financial account management, covering the transfer of investment accounts through trusts, inheritance processes, and power of attorney arrangements.\n- Discussions focus on brokerage account management, investment transaction processes, settlement fund usage, and troubleshooting technical issues with online trading platforms.\n- Discussions about tax-efficient investment strategies for selling shares, focusing on cost basis methods and brokerage procedures that minimize capital gains tax liability.\n- Discussions about persistent technical issues with investment account data synchronization between brokerage platforms and third-party financial management applications.\n- Discussions focus on the operational challenges and complexities investors face when buying, selling, and transferring mutual fund shares between different financial institutions.\n- Discussions focus on understanding and calculating tax obligations for investment income, including dividends, capital gains, and tax-exempt securities.\n- Discussions about retirement account rollover processes, customer service experiences with financial institutions, and challenges with traditional paper-based transfer methods.\n- Discussions focus on navigating investment platform interfaces to access account information and fund details, with users sharing technical solutions to overcome platform usability issues.\n- Discussions about limitations in financial account automation features, particularly regarding automatic transfers and withdrawal capabilities between different account types.\n- Discussions focus on retirement account strategies involving traditional and Roth IRA conversions, particularly backdoor Roth techniques and their associated tax implications and procedural requirements.\n\n    \nCONTEXT:\n[{'id': '1skjw0u', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1skjw0u: Why I won\\'t use Vanguard\\n\\nI tried to sign up to Vanguard for a brokerage account. It didn\\'t work. Multiple failures, frustration.\\n\\nI planned on transferring a sizeable balance to them to manage. Now I definitely won\\'t, and here\\'s why.\\n\\n* First I tried to sign up for the account. At first things were going fine. Then it trapped me in some kind of flow where it would only keep requiring me to fund the account. No login, no dashboard, no anything, just keep trying to fund the account.\\n* The funding flow continuously fails. I try to hook up Fidelity, fails. I try to hook up Schwab, fails. I try to hook up Bank of America, and at first it fails, but then when I retry it (I retried them all) magically the account is there now - *sort of* \\\\- except it\\'s sort of greyed out. I try to click something else to select funds, just get errors.\\n* So I go on Google and find Reddit threads (thanks Reddit!) where people have secret links that should help you finish adding accounts. So I use those links, and sure enough, it gets me to a normal dashboard and out of  the stupid funding flow. (wtf?? why is this the method to get in?!)\\n* I try to use one of their flows to transfer all assets from another brokerage to Vanguard. This, of course, also fails. Can\\'t transfer the Schwab assets, which are considerable. So I give up for the day.\\n* Today I tried to login to try it again. The login page is broken. Just a big stupid blank screen, not even an error. I clear my cookies/cache and try again. Nothing.\\n\\nOk then, Vanguard. You don\\'t want my money? Fine, i\\'ll go to other brokerages.\\n\\nYou\\'d think people with that much money could hire halfway competent people to run a website. If they\\'re this incompetent at basic websites, I can\\'t imagine what they\\'re doing with the funds. Big old *Nope* from me.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment og0jemb: I had a similar experience with the first bullet point.  I wanted to move a wellington mutual fund from fidelity to vanguard to pick up the admiral share version, but it wouldn\\'t let me direct it from Fidelity.  Vanguard service to assist is difficult to obtain.  I ended up bailing.  Might be for the better keeping it at Fidelity.\\n- Comment ofzxuv5: Possibilities:\\n1. The Vanguard link you initially clicked was a spoof site. Did you click the first \"Sponsored\" link that showed up in Google search? Always scroll down to the top non-sponsored link to make sure you are going to the real Vanguard.\\n2. You clicked random links in a Reddit search and proceeded to enter a bunch of account numbers into the website it took you to. \\n\\nI hope you didn\\'t get scammed.\\n    - Comment og0gwaj: Thank you for the concern but it wasn\\'t a scam, even though trying to login brings you to 3 different domains, I verified they were real domains\\n- Comment og050im: Definitely sounds like user error'}, {'id': '1rop3zf', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1rop3zf: Possibly the worst website to navigate, ever.\\n\\nNo seriously, i cant even use it(literally) each time i try to create a plan i am told to set up an online access, and in order to do so i type in my credentials as given, once that is completed, i am thrown into the login screen and told to provide a username and password( that i never set up or was given via email or anywhere else)in my confusion i clicked set up online access, in order to provide a chosen user and passcode. and once again i am met with providing the same credentials, as previously noted above but heres the tricky part, they want me to provide a number I LITERALLY NEVER TOLD OR DONT HAVE, and if i get lucky and that part is omitted for something i did set up, i move on to the next phase...which is well...a blank....screen...as shown above...\\n\\nHoly Lord someone help me.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment o9fhnpr: The worst customer service and old craggy website compared to the competition.\\n    - Comment o9fi6ps: Im gonna try fidelity at this point, it even assumes whether or not you have an active employer, even if you do not provide any surrounding details on that matter and will refuse service to those trying to register for an individual investment account(my experience as of now)\\n- Comment o9gqvdp: I kept hearing last year about the 'upcoming' rollout of a big website redesign. Anyone hear about that or where it's at?\\n    - Comment o9tntk9: It’s coming\\n- Comment o9fibjn: This is why I moved to Fidelity after a month. Couldn’t handle it.\"}, {'id': '1rfhjit', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1rfhjit: Offshore workers are fucking with your money\\n\\nso it's what it sounds like..Vanguard has paired with Infosys and now people in almost every department are offshore workers in Bangalore, who are incredibly stupid, cut corners, don't follow policy, don't understand American laws, barely speak English when we have to help them and make tons of mistakes. Worst, they aren't on camera or using real names so we don't actually know who they are. They also get caught using each other's computers and getting work without ever going through training. Just Google how many times Infosys has been sued by other companies they worked for and you'll see how bad it is. They are handling your money. And they are not handling it well. If you feel some type of way about this, please let Vanguard know. They aren't listening to those of us who have worked there for years and they announced this morning they plan on bringing more of them in. We are losing our jobs to these people and the quality/legality of your money handling is going downhill very quickly.\\n\\nEdit to add: everyone is focusing on phones. No, they don't answer the phone. But the people you talk to on the phone are not the ones handling your money. All background processing and money movement is being handled by these idiots.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment o7kjs0t: Okay... so a few questions... as a client, how should we go about saying something if Vanguard isn't listening to their own employees?  And to whom?  \\n\\nWhat assets are managed by the Vanguard/Infosys partnership?  According to Vanguard's website, it specifies corporate retirement plans.  Is that not accurate?\\n    - Comment o7nmx7d: “BlackRock executive\\xa0Salim Ramji\\xa0as its new CEO”\\xa0\\n\\nAfter that, it was inevitable.\\n- Comment o7m88sp: Very gupta. Kindly do the needful\\n- Comment o7lbccl: OP got expense ratio'd.\\n    - Comment o7lfdt1: Lolllll\"}, {'id': '1r4knn4', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1r4knn4: Looking for reviews for those who transferred from a Big Box investment house to Vanguard\\n\\nJust retired, 66, currently at a big name brokerage house for 20+ years, it has way too many fees, very much liking the Boglehead philosophy, not someone who wants to fiddle with investments. I'd love to hear from anyone who moved their 6 figure portfolio to Vanguard in a similar situation. I'm considering the Personal Advisor or Personal Advisor Select options for the first year as a way to get situated and for retirement tax planning. Anyone have experience with that move to Vanguard I would appreciate hearing your experiences. Did you go DIY, or have their team make the moves? Did you go with either Advisor option?\\n\\nI have to say that my first phone contact them 2 days ago was very unimpressive. Not the people, the actual phone quality was so bad I had to have them call me back when their office was quieter. When they did call back the connection was so bad I had to have them repeat themselves way too many times. If they can't get their phones right... it did not inspire confidence. (Fwiw my hearing is fine, on an iPhone, speaker and earphones.) Thank you.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment o5etzkt: Having been with Vanguard for decades, I finally moved over to Schwab for their customer service. I do still keep the majority of my assets in Vanguard funds/ETFs however.\\n\\nVanguard used to be quite good until they nerfed the Flagship program which had dedicated reps who were empowered to act to resolve issues and to provide genuinely good advice. Now the regular 800 agents are not empowered to do anything other than walk one through an online form that sits somewhere in the Philippines aging for about two weeks before someone pulls it off the pile. For a client-owned firm, the decay of customer service is an outright embarrassment.\\n- Comment o5ctmio: They do have some overseas call centers like many firms.  This is the likely cause of phone quality.  I think if you have a PAS advisor they will be US based, but ask.\\n\\nI dont use PAS, only DIY at Vanguard.  If I wanted help would use an independent financial planner and pay for just a plan, for a fixed fee, not annual aum related fees to have them implement the plan.\\n\\nOne economical option is planvision.\\nLook at planvisionmn.com\\n\\nVanguard is good to hold your funds while you DIY.  schwab may have better customer service from what I hear, but I would still go DIY and consider a fee only advisor, regardless of the brokerage chosen.\\n- Comment o5ctd05: My 401K and retirement are with Merrill.  My brokerage account was a DIY with Schwab.  When it was a little over a million, I moved to Vanguard **Personal Advisor Select**.  I shopped Merrill, Fidelity, Schwab, and Vanguard.  The 0.3% AUM was attractive.  All gave me a proposal first and Vanguards was the 2nd most thorough.  Merrill was the most through.  \\n\\nVanguard has developed a cashflow for now until my wife and I are 100.  It includes detailed spending, and more importantly, detailed decumulation.  The distribution phase of planning is so much more complicated than the accumulation phase.  We have a meeting every few months to review performance and what is forecasted.\\n\\nThey are video zoom calls, and I have a dedicated person assigned to me.  I am very happy with him.  He is responsive and has taken advantage of opportunities I would not have otherwise found.\\n\\nHow much you have will determine the level of support.\"}, {'id': '1qu0jjq', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1qu0jjq: Any advice for getting to actual customer service?\\n\\nIt seems to take 30 minutes of getting transferred around before I can get to someone who knows how to do their job. I'm calling about a Trust account. The call center people with terrible phone connections and who sound like they're at a party are especially unhelpful.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment o5664ej: Avoid it, it’s a hoop jumping contest, hoe many can they get you to jump through before you snap\"}, {'id': '1ojprcj', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1ojprcj: Vanguard sucks\\n\\nThey treat employees like dogs.  So, what do you think they treat vulnerable clients like?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nm66a8y: I have a friend who likes working for Vanguard.\\n- Comment nm5e6ej: This makes no sense mate, Amazon also treats their workers like dogs, but Amazon is still a good service? I think vangaurd is great, despite supposedly treating their workers like 'dogs'.\\n- Comment nm5l5m7: Source?\\n    - Comment nm6cbyo: True, where is this info coming from XD\"}]\n\nUSER QUERY:\nwhat are the top 10 complaints about this company\n\nINSTRUCTIONS:\n1. Answer the user's query based ONLY on the information provided in the context.\n2. If the user's query is not related to the context, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n3. If the context doesn't contain enough information to answer the query, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n4. Do not make up information or use knowledge outside of the provided context.\n5. For every piece of evidence you cite, add a reference at the end of the sentence in the format [postID] where postID is the exact ID of the source document.\n6. Example of proper reference: \"Users are experiencing battery life issues with the device [qpmkvr].\"\n7. Make sure all postID references are accurate and correspond to actual post IDs from the provided context.\n8. Do not modify the postID format - keep it exactly as shown in the context (do not add extra characters or modify the ID).\n9. Organize your response using markdown section headers (##) to categorize different perspectives.\n10. Use headers like \"## Positive Perspectives\", \"## Criticisms\", \"## Feature Requests\", \"## Common Issues\", etc. as appropriate for the query.\n11. Under each section header, provide a bulleted list explaining the key points with references.\n\n\nANSWER:\n"